Revision history for Skilletor

{{$NEXT}}
  - `skilletor add [name] <spec> [--project]` in the Perl CLI
    (Skilletor::Commands::add, after cmdAdd): the same checks in the same
    order - the k95 name, no project scope, the spec, the k110 definition, the
    k101 taken name, the k119 ref, the k126 path, the k116 kept entry. It then
    trusts the source and syncs, prints the added or kept line, and prints the
    k103 saved lines when the sync fails (k176).
  - New Skilletor::Probe: `git ls-remote -- url` (5 s), then an in-process
    HEAD on url.tar.gz through HTTP::Tiny with fetch's headers, so a packed
    binary needs neither Node nor Perl for it. Tested in t/31-probe.t (k176).
  - The Codex target in the Perl engine: sync, check and status install
    skills in .agents/skills, agents as TOML and rules as sections of one
    skilletor-rules.md with the pointer block in the Codex AGENTS.md - its
    warnings (malformed markers, a link, a directory or Claude Code's
    CLAUDE.md in its place, AGENTS.override.md, project_doc_max_bytes) and the
    migration of a first-design rules block as the reference has them - and
    warn while Codex has not trusted skilletor's SessionStart hook
    (Skilletor::AgentsMD, after agentsmd.ts; Skilletor::Error::Block). A
    scope that targets Hermes is still refused. The hooks under --harness
    codex: a new session's context starts with the rules files, and the
    prompt hook syncs the checkout a session moved into, its answer joined
    with a pending report (k184).
  - `skilletor trust`, `skilletor source list [--json]` and `skilletor source
    remove <name> [--project] [--force]` in the Perl CLI (Skilletor::Commands,
    after the reference's commands.ts): the same checks in the same order (an
    undeclared name and where else it is declared, an entry other files'
    overrides still need, the one entry config load refuses removed, installed
    items without --force), the same messages and exit codes, the config
    rewritten as two-space JSON in its key order. Skilletor::Config gains
    has_source_entry (an entry written as null is declared). New conformance
    cases 72 to 82 hold both CLIs to the same bytes.
  - t/54-parity-cli.t runs the reference CLI (node dist/skilletor.js) and
    bin/skilletor on one machine, in the same paths, and holds the port to the
    same bytes: exit codes, both streams, installed trees, the lock and the
    state files (render-inputs, sources-read, unreached, the hooks' marks and
    records), over five conformance cases, git's failure wording, the real
    host and user in templates, and one sync lock that either CLI holds while
    the other's sync and SessionStart wait or time out. It skips without node
    or the bundle.
  - Skilletor::Process runs programs on Windows: stdin, stdout and stderr as
    temp files on the standard handles of a P_NOWAIT spawn, the program found
    on PATH as Node finds it (never in the cwd first, never a .bat or .cmd),
    its arguments quoted for the C runtime, a timeout terminating it
    (reported as signal 15, as Node does there); spawn_detached starts the
    hooks' background through Win32::Process (new Windows-only dependency)
    with no inherited handle and a console without a window.
    Skilletor::State refreshes the sync lock on Windows while it waits on a
    program, and stamps times with the core utime there (Time::HiRes has
    none). Untested on real Windows (exercised on Linux through an injected
    seam).
  - `skilletor hook cwd-changed|post-tool-use` for Claude Code: a session
    that moves into another checkout gets that checkout's project scope
    synced – its git top level, never CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR – when its lock no
    longer matches its config (a local check, no source asked), with a 20 s
    deadline past which nothing is written and a wait on the sync lock up to
    it; one line names the checkout and what to run there, PostToolUse adds
    a context for the model. New conformance cases 60 to 64 hold both CLIs
    to the same bytes.
  - `skilletor hook session-start|user-prompt-submit` for Claude Code: the
    SessionStart check and sync, the prompt's interval check with a detached
    background sync (a packed binary re-runs itself via PAR_PROGNAME), and
    pending reports. Every failure is one warning line with exit 0, and a
    prompt with nothing due never loads the engine. New conformance cases 50
    to 59 hold both CLIs to the same hook output.
  - Renders Nunjucks templates as the reference does, through Text::Nunjucks
    (a Perl port of Nunjucks 3.2.4, not on CPAN yet; new dependency, Perl
    5.40): a sync installs .njk items rendered with the reference's context,
    skips an item whose main template renders empty and words template errors
    the same. resolve_skill and its siblings still refuse a templated item.
  - The sync, check and status reports keep the reference's key order, and
    Skilletor::Report's new report_json writes them byte for byte as
    skilletor sync|check|status --json prints them.
  - A command line, bin/skilletor over Skilletor::CLI: the reference's usage
    text, version (the plugin's, provisional), option checks, usage errors and
    exit codes, byte for byte; a command not ported yet says so and exits 1.
    Skilletor::Process::run_command feeds stdin through a
    pipe when given one.
  - The engine options the hooks set, as the reference has them: check's local
    (no source asked), the sync's if_changed (a local check under the sync
    lock), deadline and a per-call lock_timeout_ms. Skilletor::State keeps
    pending-report.json and session-cwd.json (the 256 sessions recorded last)
    byte for byte as the reference does, and gains due_again;
    Skilletor::Report's new report_hook words a sync report as the hooks do.
    report_text gives an item with an unknown harness prefix its type's
    activation hint.
  - Skilletor::Convert and Skilletor::TOML port the reference's Codex
    conversion: an agent as a Codex agent-role TOML file (the codex:
    passthrough, its warnings, the # briefing: skills line), a rule as its
    section of the Codex rules file, and the per-target mapping of an item's
    output, byte for byte; not yet used by a sync.
  - The CLI runs sync, check and status as the reference does: the report
    text and --json, check's exit 0, 1 or 2, status with the target naming,
    --scope, --force and --project-dir; the project root is the git top level
    of the working directory (2 s, else the directory itself). The
    conformance runner's cli steps hold it to the reference's bytes.
  - scripts/ builds bin/skilletor into one standalone executable with
    PAR::Packer (build-binary.sh), proves it byte for byte against perl -Ilib
    (verify-binary.sh), checks its shared libraries and glibc floor
    (check-binary-libs.sh), and runs the shared conformance cases' cli steps
    against it in a container with neither perl nor node (container-proof.sh);
    scripts/ stays out of the CPAN tarball.
  - scripts/smoke-binary.sh runs a packed binary through a sync, check and
    status of the t/data/smoke fixture on a clean machine without Perl
    (t/70-smoke-binary.t), and the Claude Code hooks, the detached
    background sync included (k205); verify-binary.sh now also works with
    git before 2.32. scripts/loaded-modules.pl packs Win32::Process on
    MSWin32 and runs on Windows, and the release-binaries Windows leg runs the
    full smoke (k210).

0.001     2026-10-24 03:08:52Z
  - Follows the reference's k145: CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR is the user scope's
    .claude (config, lock, roots, state dir, markers, briefing lookup); a
    source's non-object vars, a lock via that is no array of strings and a
    truncated url tarball are errors.
  - Initial release: the Perl port of skilletor, beside the TypeScript
    reference in the same repository, sharing its config files, its
    ~/.claude/skilletor/cache and its trust.json.
  - Skilletor::Config loads the user, project and local config with the
    reference's merge and precedence rules (per-field source merge with
    origins, author mode, entries without a backend, "ref": null, vars,
    gitignore, claudeSettings, targets), reports the same errors in the same
    order, and edits a config file as `add`/`install`/`uninstall` do.
  - Skilletor::Entry parses [type:]name@source, patterns and
    [bundle:]name@source; Skilletor::Spec resolves `add` shorthands (forge
    links, scp-like and transport::address forms, probed generic hosts).
  - Sources: Skilletor::Source::Local, ::Git (shallow fetch, refs, SHA pins,
    offline cache reuse, repair and restore after killed runs, lock-file
    sweep) and ::Url (https tarball, ETag or sha256 version, staged swap,
    sweep). Skilletor::Cache names cache directories as the reference does,
    so both implementations share one fetch.
  - Skilletor::Backend picks a source's backend (author mode) for trust and
    fetch alike; Skilletor::State reads and writes trust.json;
    Skilletor::Targets detects harnesses and merges targets.
  - Skilletor::JSON parses like JSON.parse (key order, V8's messages) and
    writes like JSON.stringify(value, null, 2), byte for byte;
    Skilletor::URL, ::Gzip, ::Tar, ::JSString and ::FSUtil carry the Node
    behaviour that reaches a name, a byte or a message.
  - Skilletor resolves skills, agents and rules as a sync would install
    them, without installing: resolve_skill, resolve_agent, resolve_rule
    and resolve_rules give the main file in the source tree or the shared
    cache, resolve_item, resolve_items and items the whole record. Both
    scopes declaring a name follow Claude Code's precedence: the personal
    skill wins, the project's agent wins, rules of both scopes apply (the
    user's first) – resolve_rule throws Skilletor::Error::Ambiguous rather
    than hide one. fetch => always, missing or never
    picks the network use; no agent harness is needed (require_harness
    restores the reference's check). An item with a .njk file is flagged
    templated and refused as an include (Skilletor::Error::Unsupported):
    the port renders no templates.
  - Skilletor::Resolver ports the reference's resolveItems: trust, sources
    into the cache, catalogs, wildcards, bundles with their vars and
    entries of other sources, the overlap rules and the lock's kept
    offers, with the reference's warnings and report, under its sync lock.
  - Skilletor::Catalog scans a source tree (layout, plugin.json, the
    Claude project layout, a source's path, item errors),
    Skilletor::Bundles parses and expands bundle files, and
    Skilletor::Frontmatter reads the reference's YAML subset, with its
    error messages. Skilletor::Lock reads lock files.
  - Skilletor::State takes the sync lock (sync.lock/) as the reference
    does, so the two exclude each other; the git, url and local sources
    gain cached, their cache without the network.
  - Skilletor::Tar refuses a header whose size field is no non-negative
    number as a corrupt archive ("corrupt tar entry (size): <name>"), as
    the reference now does: a url source falls back to its cache.
  - Skilletor->sync, ->check and ->status (Skilletor::Engine) port the
    reference's commands for the Claude Code target: the same installed
    files and executable bits, lock, .gitignore blocks, .worktreeinclude
    and .claude/settings.json plugin requirement (k140), conflicts and
    force, removal of what is no longer declared, partial entries after a
    failed write, the briefing check, the same JSON reports and the same
    state records (render-inputs.json, sources-read.json, unreached.json),
    so either implementation's check accepts what the other synced. A
    scope targeting Codex or Hermes, or a sync where the CLI left their
    state, is refused with nothing touched. A .njk item is never installed
    unrendered: the sync warns, keeps what the CLI installed, and leaves the
    scope open for the CLI's next sync.
  - Skilletor::Apply, ::Render (copy only), ::Gitignore, ::ClaudeSettings,
    ::Briefing and ::Report port apply.ts, the copy half of render.ts,
    gitignore.ts, claudesettings.ts, briefing.ts and report.ts (with the
    CLI's status text and exit codes); Skilletor::Lock writes locks,
    Skilletor::State keeps the records check reads, Skilletor::Targets has
    the layout table, Skilletor::JSON takes a string indent.
  - t/60-conformance.t runs every step of the shared conformance cases;
    t/52-parity-sync.t has the reference and the port sync one machine and
    compares every byte they leave.
  - Tests replay oracles recorded from the reference (t/data) and, in a
    checkout with node, drive the reference and the port against the same
    remotes and cache (t/50-parity.t).
